{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794739048,"nonce":"9X36Fe8f7wZaAHmkNEfz9RjUCSI=","title":"An hxn call on an object spends most of its time on the handle","message":"On every compiled runtime, a call from Haxe into Rust costs about 1-3 ns bare and 7-10 ns when it takes an object, and the difference is hxn's handle: `Handle::borrow`/`borrow_mut` load the magic, compare the TypeId, and take and release the borrow with an atomic compare-exchange and a store. The runtime makes no difference: Ash tiered, Ash's native build and hxcpp measure the same. For a GPU driver this is the per-command cost, paid on every encoder call.\n\nMeasured on 2026-11-15 on an Apple-silicon Mac, Rust in release, ns per call, median of 7 samples of 1,000,000 calls:\n\n| Runtime | Haxe call (no crossing) | Static, no arguments | Method on a handle (\u0026self) | Int in and out (\u0026mut self) | 16 bytes in (\u0026self) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ash, tiered | 2.2 | 2.1 | 9.9-10.0 | 7.3-7.5 | 9.9-10.3 |\n| Ash, native build | 2.2 | 1.0 | 10.0 | 7.3 | 10.3 |\n| hxcpp | 0 (optimized away) | 2.9 | 9.8 | 7.3 | 9.8 |\n| CPPIA | 26.8 | 12.9 | 17.5 | 19.3 | 22.5 |\n\nA shared borrow (`\u0026self`) costs more than an exclusive one (`\u0026mut self`), likely the compare-exchange loop against a single compare-exchange; not profiled. Places to look, none tried: the order and memory orderings of the checks, a cheaper path for the shared borrow, and whether the TypeId compare can move to where a handle enters a call rather than every call. The checks are what make a closed, foreign or wrongly typed handle fail instead of crashing, and Busy what catches re-entry, so any change keeps those failures.\n\nThe benchmark (an hxn library with `Probe` for these calls and `Pass` for Metal draws, see the hxn-gpu issue) is in the comment below. Run it with `hxn run hl --release`, `hxn run cpp --release`, `hxn run cppia --release`, and `ash --build` on its main.hl.","files":null}]}